  "ai_language_tip": "English is official or widely used in all eight and is how offices, hotels and shops will answer a call; Samoan, Tongan, Gilbertese, Marshallese and Palauan are not on Bageo's list.",
  "english_ok": "mostly — Fine in Apia, Nuku'alofa, Koror, Majuro and Pohnpei businesses; outer-island stores and older shopkeepers may prefer the local language.",
  "politeness": "Soft-spoken and respectful; greet properly (talofa in Samoa, malo e lelei in Tonga) and do not hurry; family and church come first.",
  "name_tip": "Many people go by one given name; Samoan chiefly titles may precede a name; spell each name out letter by letter.",
  "phone_tip": "Numbers are short: Samoa mobiles are 7 digits starting 7, Tongan numbers 5–7 digits, most other islands 7 digits; there is no trunk zero; read as 3+4.",
  "time_tip": "12-hour speech with am/pm; Samoa and Tonga sit at UTC+13, Kiribati's Line Islands at UTC+14, Palau at UTC+9, so confirm which island's clock.",
  "number_tip": "Currencies differ: tala (Samoa), pa'anga (Tonga), Australian dollar (Kiribati, Nauru, Tuvalu), US dollar (Micronesia, Marshall Islands, Palau); name it with each sum.",
  "avoid_calling": "Do not call on Sunday, which Tonga's law keeps as a day of rest and Samoa treats as church and family time; avoid evening prayer hours in Samoan villages.",
  "closed_days": "Offices close Saturday and Sunday; in Tonga almost all business stops on Sunday; independence and church holidays close government counters.",
  "booking_channels": "Facebook pages and Messenger carry most bookings, with phone calls for small stores; flights and inter-island boats are booked at their offices or websites; payment is mostly cash or EFTPOS.",
  "voice_menu": "Telcos (Digicel, Vodafone, local state carriers) have brief English IVR; most businesses answer directly.",
  "callback_habits": "Shops reply on Messenger when they can; inter-island operators announce schedule changes on Facebook rather than calling back.",
